alt="Node.js" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/nodejs.svg" width="16"/> Node.js</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/spring-boot-tutorial"><img alt="Spring Boot" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/spring-boot.svg" width="16"/> Spring Boot</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/c-sharp-tutorial"><img alt="c#" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/csharp.svg" width="16"/> C#</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/php-tutorial"><img alt="php" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/PHP.svg" width="16"/> PHP</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/mysql-tutorial"><img alt="MySQL" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/mysql.svg" width="16"/> MySQL</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/mongodb-tutorial"><img alt="MongoDB" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/mongodb.svg" width="16"/> MongoDB</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/artificial-intelligence-ai"><img alt="Artificial Intelligence" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/AI.svg" width="16"/> AI</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/machine-learning"><img alt="Machine Learning" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/ML.svg" width="16"/> ML</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/data-structures-and-algorithms-dsa"><img alt="data structures" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/DS.svg" width="16"/> DSA</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/dbms-tutorial"><img alt="dbms" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/DBMS.svg" width="16"/> DBMS</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/operating-system"><img alt="Operating System" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/OS.svg" width="16"/> OS</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/aptitude/quantitative"><img alt="aptitude" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/Aptitude.svg" width="16"/> Aptitude</a></li><li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="/reasoning"><img alt="reasoning" height="16" src="https://tpointtech-images.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/static/images/Reasoning.svg" width="16"/> Reasoning</a></li></ul></div></div></div><section class="course-details"><div class="container-fluid"><div class="row"><div class="col-md-2 sidebar" id="sidebar"><div id="menu-sidebar"><div class="accordion accordion-flush" id="accordionFlushExample"><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading0"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse0" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse0" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Jupyter Notebook</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading0" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse0"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/jupyter-notebook">Jupyter Notebook</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/jupyter-notebook-shortcuts">Jupyter Notebook Shortcuts</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="servlet-tutorial">next →</a> <a class="next" href="jupyter-notebook">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Jupyter Notebook Shortcuts</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>23 Jan 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 10 min read</span></div><h2 class="h2">Introduction</h2><p>A popular tool for collaborative computing in data science, artificial intelligence, and scholarly study is Jupyter Notebook. Become proficient with Jupyter Notebook keyboard shortcuts to greatly increase productivity by automating repetitive processes and decreasing reliance on the mouse. There are two main modes of operation for this interface: Command Mode as well as Edit Mode. Each mode has its own set of shortcuts that are specific to certain tasks.</p><p>Shortcuts in Command Mode make managing cells and navigating the notebook easier. For example, hitting 'A' or 'B' adds additional cells above or beneath the one that is now selected, 'X' cuts a cell, and 'Z' reverses the previous operation. For effective notebook organisation and structure, Command Mode is recommended.</p><p>Edit Mode is centred on creating and editing material and is accessed by clicking 'Enter' on a cell. Here, keyboard shortcuts like 'Ctrl + /' for activating remarks and 'Tab' for code formatting aid in text and code editing. Writing and editing code or content within a cell is made easier using Edit Mode.</p><h2 class="h2">How to Switch Between Modes</h2><h3 class="h3">Comparing Command and Edit Modes</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Mode of Command:</strong><br/><strong>Goal:</strong> Enables users to carry out notebook-level tasks, including controlling cell contents, switching between cell kinds, and moving between cells.<br/><strong>Initiation:</strong> To go from Edit Mode into Command Mode, press Esc.<br/><strong>Significance:</strong> The border of the cell is blue.</li><li><strong>Edit Setting:</strong><br/><strong>Goal:</strong> Allows users to change text in a cell, including raw, markdown, and code.<br/><strong>Initiation:</strong> When a cell is selected, press Enter to go from Command Mode into Edit Mode.<br/><strong>Significance:</strong> The border of the cell is green.</li><li><strong>Changing Modes</strong><br/><strong>Switching from Command to Edit Mode:</strong><ul class="points"><li>Enter now : To begin modifying the cell that is presently selected, press Enter.</li><li>Click with the mouse: To get straight into Edit Mode, click within a cell.</li></ul><strong>Switching from Edit to Command Mode:</strong><ul class="points"><li>Esc:To exit modifying the cell and go back to Command Mode, press Esc.</li><li>Press and hold the M key: An additional method for switching from Edit Mode to Command Mode.</li></ul></li></ul><h3 class="h3">Frequently Used Shortcuts in Mode Navigation</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Enter now:</strong> Switch from Command to Edit Mode.</li><li><strong>Esc:</strong> Switch from Edit Mode with Command Mode.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Getting Around in Command Mode in Cells</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Keys Arr (↑ and ↓):</strong> Navigate vertically within cells.</li><li><strong>J or K:</strong> Go up or down in between cells.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Useful Advice</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Fast Switching:</strong> To go between managing cells and typing swiftly, use Enter along with Esc often.</li><li><strong>Visual Indications:</strong> To determine your current mode, look at the colour of the cell border (green for edit mode, blue for command mode).</li><li><strong>Effectiveness:</strong> Regular practice of the shortcuts will make you more proficient and less dependent on the mouse.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Shortcuts in Command Mode</h2><h3 class="h3">Fundamental Navigation</h3><ul class="points"><li>Move towards the cell above with the up <strong>arrow (↑).</strong></li><li>Move to a cell below with the <strong>down arrow (↓).</strong></li><li><strong>K:</strong> Proceed to the higher cell.</li><li><strong>J:</strong> Proceed to the lower cell.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Cellular Functions</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>A:</strong> Place a fresh cell atop the one that is there.</li><li><strong>B:&lt;</strong> Add a new cell underneath the one that is there.</li><li><strong>X:</strong> Slice the chosen cell.</li><li><strong>C:</strong> Duplicate the chosen cell.</li><li><strong>V:</strong> Insert the cell beneath.</li><li>Press <strong>Shift + V</strong> to paste the previous cell.</li><li><strong>D, D:</strong> Eliminate the chosen cell.</li><li><strong>Z:</strong> Revert the previous cell deletion.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Altering Cell Types</h3><ul class="points"><li>Select <strong>Y</strong> to convert the cell you have selected to a numerical code cell.</li><li><strong>M:</strong> Convert the chosen cell into a Markdown format.</li><li><strong>R:</strong> Select the cell and set its format to Raw.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Activating Cells</h3><ul class="points"><li>To run the currently selected cell and go on to the next, press <strong>Shift + Enter.</strong></li><li>Enter with <strong>Ctrl</strong> to run the present cell.</li><li>Press <strong>Alt + Enter</strong> to run the currently selected cell and add a new one underneath it.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Additional Practical Shortcuts</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Esc:</strong> Switch to Command Mode.</li><li><strong>Enter:</strong> Switch to the Edit Mode.</li><li>To scroll the notepad up, use <strong>Shift + Space.</strong></li><li><strong>Space:</strong> Lower the notepad by scrolling.</li><li><strong>0, 0:</strong> Refresh the kernel (after a prompt for confirmation).</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Cell Selection</h3><ul class="points"><li>To extend your choice to the cell above, use <strong>Shift + Up Arrow (↑).</strong></li><li>Slide the cursor down to the cell below using <strong>Shift + Down Arrow (↑).</strong></li></ul><h3 class="h3">Cell Functions</h3><ul class="points"><li>Use <strong>Ctrl + S</strong> to checkpoint and save the notebook.</li><li><strong>L:</strong> Change the line numbers within the chosen cell.</li><li><strong>O:</strong> Toggle the chosen cell's output.</li><li>Toggle the chosen cell's output scrolling by pressing <strong>Shift + O.</strong></li></ul><h3 class="h3">Advice on Efficient Utilisation</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Recall Important Shortcuts:</strong> To improve productivity, become familiar with the most often used shortcuts.</li><li><strong>Practice:</strong> By using these shortcuts frequently, you'll become accustomed to them and accelerate your productivity.</li><li><strong>Personalise:</strong> If the built-in shortcuts don't work for your workflow, you may modify them using the Jupyter Notebook interface.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Edit Mode Shortcuts</h2><h3 class="h3">Basics of Text Editing</h3><ul class="points"><li>Use the <strong>arrow keys</strong> to move about the cell.</li><li>To move the mouse to the beginning of the preceding word, press <strong>Ctrl + Left Arrows (←).</strong></li><li>Press and hold Control to move the cursor to the beginning of the following word <strong>(→).</strong></li><li><strong>Home:</strong> Position the cursor at the start of the line.</li><li><strong>End:</strong> Position the cursor at the line's end.</li><li>Use <strong>Ctrl + A</strong> to select every word in the cell.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Coding formatting and indentation</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Tab:</strong> Add a space after the chosen line or code block.</li><li>Use <strong>Shift + Tab</strong> to lessen the amount of indentation.</li><li>Text Copies, Cuts, and Pastes:<ul class="points"><li>Copy the chosen text using <strong>Ctrl + C.</strong></li><li><strong>Ctrl + X:</strong> Select and cut text.</li><li>Paste text with <strong>Ctrl + V.</strong></li></ul></li></ul><h3 class="h3">Shortcuts for Multi-Cursor and Selection</h3><ul class="points"><li>Use <strong>Ctrl + Click</strong> to place a new cursor where you clicked.</li><li>Drag and drop to make numerous selections using <strong>Ctrl + Click.</strong></li><li>Use the <strong>Ctrl, Shift,</strong> and <strong>Arrow Keys</strong> to select multiple items.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Markdown Cell Quick Links</h3><ul class="points"><li>Press <strong>Ctrl + 1</strong> through <strong>Ctrl + 6</strong> to create headers at various levels.</li><li><strong>Ctrl + B</strong> to make text bold.</li><li>To italicise text, use <strong>Ctrl + I.</strong></li><li>To make an unordered list, use <strong>Ctrl + L.</strong></li><li>Use <strong>Ctrl + Shift + L</strong> to arrange items in a list.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Additional Practical Shortcuts</h3><ul class="points"><li>Toggle comments on the chosen line or code block by pressing <strong>Ctrl + /.</strong></li><li><strong>Ctrl + Z:</strong> Reverse the previous action.</li><li>Use <strong>Ctrl + Y</strong> to undo the previous action.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Useful Advice</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Consistent Practice:</strong> In order to become adept, use these shortcuts frequently.</li><li><strong>Personalisation:</strong> If necessary, you may adjust shortcuts using the Jupyter Notebook settings.</li><li><strong>Visual Signals:</strong> Take advantage of visual clues such as text that is highlighted and cursor locations to better comprehend the consequences of shortcuts.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Installing Required Libraries</h2><h3 class="h3">1. Setting up PuLP</h3><p>PuLP is a Python library for linear and integer programming that is available to the public.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">pulp install pip 
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">2. Setting up OR-Tools on Google</h3><p>A powerful collection of open-source optimisation tools is Google OR-Tools. It has solvers for many optimisation issues, such as integer and linear programming.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">pip install ortools 
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">3. Setting up CPLEX</h3><p>A high-performance solution for quadratic, mixed-integer, and linear programming is called CPLEX. It is an IBM commercial product.</p><p><strong>Steps for Installation:</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Download:</strong> Visit IBM's website to get IBM ILOG CPLEX Optimisation Studio.</li><li><strong>Install:</strong> Comply with your operating system's installation guidelines.</li><li><strong>Python API:</strong> Download the Python API following the installation of CPLEX.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">4. Setting up Gurobi</h3><p>Another high-performance solution for mixed-integer, linear, and quadratic programming is called Gourbi. A licence is needed, however academic licences are given away for free.</p><p><strong>Steps for Installation:</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Install and download:</strong> To find and set up the programme, rendering to the guidelines providing on the Gourbi website.</li><li><strong>Download</strong> the Gourbi Python component to usage the Python API.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Time-Saving Tips and Tricks</h2><h3 class="h3">Learn How to Use Keyboard Shortcuts</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Remember Mutual Shortcuts:</strong> Learn the standard commands for both Commanding Mode (A to add a region above, B to add a unit below) and Edit Mode (Ctrl + Returning to run a region, Shift + Enter to display and proceed to the next cell).</li><li><strong>Make use of the Crosscut Help Menu:</strong> Press H to bring up the help menu complete with shortcuts while in Command Mode.</li><li><strong>Efficient Cell Delivery:</strong> Shift + M may be utilised to merge a group of cells, whereas Ctrl + Shift + - can be performed to split a cell at the reference point.</li><li><strong>Fast Conversion of Cell Types:</strong> R is for raw text, Y is for code, and M stands for markdown.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Running and Coming Back Up</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Run All Cells:</strong> To run every cell in the notepad at once, select Run All from the Run Every Cell menu.</li><li><strong>Restart Kernel and execute All:</strong> This option, which can be found underneath Kernel &gt; Restart &amp; Run All, allows you to restart the kernel and execute every cell to make sure the notebook starts up clean.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Controlling Code and Output</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Change the Line Numbers:</strong> Press L in Command The mode to flip the line number in coding cells. This facilitates troubleshooting and helps identify individual lines.</li><li><strong>Eliminate Every Output:</strong> To eliminate all cell outputs and reduce notebook size, select Kernel &gt; Restart as well as Clear Output. This will improve readability.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Boosting Output</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Employ Markdown for Documentation:</strong> When recording your code in your notebooks, use clear, succinct markdown language cells to make the text easier to read and comprehend.</li><li>Common tasks may be accomplished with IPython magic commands. For example, you may install extensions with %load_ext, watch code execution with %time it, and display graphs inline using %matplotlib inside.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Data handling and visualisation</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Interactive Widgets:</strong> For improved data exploration and visualisation, use Jupyter widgets (via Ip widgets) to build interactive features in your notebook.</li><li><strong>Pandas Display Options:</strong> To improve the visibility of your data frames, adjust the pandas display options. For example, you may show additional rows by using pd.set_option('display.max_rows', 100).</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Setting and Tailoring</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Employ Virtual Environments:</strong> Maintain a clean and repeatable notebook environment by isolating your project dependencies using simulated environments.</li><li><strong>Customise the Interface:</strong> To enhance the user interface and add functionality, utilise Jupyter extensions (extensions). The Table of the Contents, Variable The inspector, and Snippets are popular additions.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Testing and Debugging</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Interactive Debugging:</strong> To start an interactive debugging session, use the %debug magic command following an error.</li><li><strong>Assertions and Tests:</strong> To check outputs and identify mistakes early on, include assertions and basic tests in your code.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Version Management</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Monitor modifications with Git:</strong> Use git to version manage the notebooks. For improved notebook diffing and merging, think about using nbdime.</li><li><strong>Automate using Pre-commit Hooks:</strong> Make sure notes are structured correctly before committing by using pre-commit hooks to uphold coding standards.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Troubleshooting Shortcuts</h2><h3 class="h3">Not Working Shortcuts</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Verify Mode:</strong> Make sure the keyboard shortcut you are attempting to utilise is in the appropriate mode (Edit Mode or Command Mode). For Command Mode, press Esc; for Edit Mode, press Enter.</li><li><strong>Problems with Shortcuts in Browsers:</strong> Jupyter Notebook shortcuts may be superseded by certain browser shortcuts. Try switching to an alternate browser or turning off any incompatible browser add-ons.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Shortcuts Are Missing or Changed</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Return to the Initial State:</strong> Shortcuts may occasionally be inadvertently altered. To reset them, select Help &gt; Edit Keypad Shortcuts, then click each shortcut's Reset button.</li><li><strong>Reinstall Jupyter:</strong> Considering reinstalling Jupyter Notebook in order to restore everything to default if shortcuts are still giving you trouble.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Not Working Custom Shortcuts</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Appropriate Format:</strong> Make sure your customised keyboard shortcuts are structured appropriately in the custom.js file or through the Help &gt; Edit Keyboard Shortcuts menu of the Jupyter Notebook interface.</li><li><strong>Relaunch Jupyter Note:</strong> Restarting Jupyter Notebook after configuring custom shortcuts will make the modifications take effect.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Unpredictable Behaviour</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Jupyter Notebook update:</strong> Verify that Jupyter Notebook is the most recent version you are using. Update using conda update notebook or pip install --upgrade notebook.</li><li><strong>Empty the Browser's Cache:</strong> Resolving discrepancies occasionally involves cleaning the browser's cache.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Taking Back the Default Shortcuts</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Through the Interface:</strong> Navigate to Help &gt; Keyboard Shortcuts Edit.<br/>To restore all shortcuts to their initial configurations, click your Restore Defaults button.</li><li><strong>Resetting by Hand:</strong> To return to the default shortcuts, erase or comment out any customisations you may have made in the custom.js file. After making these adjustments, restart Jupyter Notebook.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Handling Disagreements</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Recognising Conflicts:</strong> Verify whether any other programmes or browser extensions are making use of the same shortcuts that Jupyter Notebook uses.</li><li>Either in Jupyter or the incompatible programme, disable or remap the conflicting shortcuts.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Taking Back the Default Shortcuts</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Through the Interface:</strong> Navigate to Help &gt; Keyboard Shortcuts Edit.<br/>To restore all shortcuts to their initial configurations, click your Restore Defaults button.</li><li><strong>Resetting by Hand:</strong> To return to the default shortcuts, erase or comment out any customisations you may have made in the custom.js file. After making these adjustments, restart Jupyter Notebook.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Handling Disagreements</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Recognising Conflicts:</strong> Verify whether any other programmes or browser extensions are making use of the same shortcuts that Jupyter Notebook uses.</li><li>Either in Jupyter or the incompatible programme, disable or remap the conflicting shortcuts.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Conclusion</h2><p>Getting proficient with Jupyter Notebook keyboard shortcuts is crucial for increasing productivity and simplifying your workflow. Knowing the shortcuts for both Edit Mode and Command Mode may greatly increase productivity by decreasing the amount of time you spend using the mouse and expediting routine operations. Shortcut problems, though, can reduce this effectiveness.</p><p>Make sure that are in the right mode for the planned shortcuts and look for problems with browser extensions or shortcuts in order to troubleshoot efficiently. Numerous problems may be solved by going back to the original settings and personalising the shortcuts using the Jupyter interface. Consistent behaviour may also be preserved by updating Jupyter Notebook and cleaning the caches on your browser.</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="servlet-tutorial">What is Servlet</a></span></div><br/><br/><div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="jupyter-notebook" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="servlet-tutorial" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br/><br/></td></tr></tbody></table></div></body></html><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="jupyter-notebook"><h3 class="h3">Jupyter Notebook</h3><p class="related-post-content">Tutorial tutorial provides basic and advanced concepts of the . Our tutorial is designed for beginner